.TH NROFF 1 2026-10-22 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
nroff \- format manual pages for a terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nroff
.RB [ \-man " | " \-mandoc " | " \-mdoc ]
.RB [ \-t ]
.RB [ \-Tascii " | " \-Tutf8 ]
[\fB\-rLL=\fIN\fBn\fR]
[\fB\-r\fIreg\fB=\fIN\fR]
[\fB\-d\fIname\fB=\fIstring\fR]
.RB [ \-w ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nroff
formats manual pages written with the man(7) or mdoc(7) macros,
including
.B tbl
tables, as text for a terminal.
It is w64devkit's own formatter, a self-contained replacement for the
pipeline of
.BR tbl ,
groff
.BR nroff ,
and
.BR col .
.BR man (1)
runs it with the page on standard input.
.PP
.B nroff
reads each
.I file
in turn, or standard input when there are none or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- ,
and writes the formatted text on standard output.
The files form one input, so definitions carry over from one file to the
next, and all of them should use the same macro package.
The macro package is detected from the prologue: mdoc(7) when
.BR .Dd ,
.BR .Dt ,
or
.B .Os
comes before any
.BR .TH ,
and man(7) otherwise.
.PP
The output is a header line, the body, and a footer line, laid out like
groff's continuous rendering, with no page breaks.
Text is filled and never hyphenated: a line breaks only at a space,
after a hyphen between two letters, or at a break point written in the
page.
A hyphen in the arguments of an mdoc(7) macro is not a break point.
With
.BR \-Tutf8 ,
a line also breaks after an em dash written as
.B \e(em
between two letters.
man(7) pages are adjusted to both margins, as in groff, unless the page
asks otherwise or
.B \-dAD=l
is given; mdoc(7) pages are left-aligned, as in mandoc.
.PP
Bold text is written between the SGR escape sequences
.B ESC[1m
and
.BR ESC[22m ,
and italic text is underlined, between
.B ESC[4m
and
.BR ESC[24m .
Each output line closes its own sequences, so every line displays
correctly on its own.
View the output with
.BR "less \-R" ,
which passes the sequences through.
Emphasis is turned off when
.B GROFF_NO_SGR
is set, and when standard output is a console in which virtual terminal
processing cannot be enabled, such as on Windows XP.
.B nroff
never writes backspace overstrikes.
.SS Roff language
These parts of the roff language are supported:
.IP \(bu 2
control lines beginning with
.B .\&
or with
.BR \(aq ,
which keeps requests such as
.B br
and
.B sp
from breaking the line, joining lines with
.B \e
at the end of a line, and comments with
.B \e"
and
.BR \e# ;
.IP \(bu 2
macros with
.BR de ,
.BR de1 ,
.BR am ,
and
.BR am1 ,
with arguments
.B \e$0
through
.BR \e$9 ,
\fB\e$[\fIn\fB]\fR,
.BR \e$* ,
.BR \e$@ ,
and
.BR \en(.$ ,
and
.BR shift ;
.IP \(bu 2
strings with
.BR ds ,
.BR ds1 ,
.BR as ,
and
.BR as1 ;
registers with
.BR nr ,
including increments; and
.BR rm ,
.BR rr ,
.BR rn ,
and
.BR als ;
.IP \(bu 2
conditionals with
.BR if ,
.BR ie ,
and
.BR el ,
including blocks in
.B \e{
and
.BR \e} ,
negation with
.BR ! ,
the conditions
.BR n ,
.BR o ,
.BR t ,
.BR v ,
.BR e ,
.BR h ,
.BR d ,
.BR r ,
.BR c ,
.BR m ,
.BR F ,
and
.BR S ,
string comparisons, and numeric expressions;
.IP \(bu 2
the requests
.BR ig ,
.BR do ,
.BR nop ,
.BR tr ,
.BR so ,
.BR br ,
.BR sp ,
.BR ce ,
.BR ta ,
.BR ti ,
.BR in ,
.BR ll ,
.BR nf ,
.BR fi ,
.BR ad ,
.BR na ,
.BR ns ,
.BR rs ,
.BR ss ,
.BR tl ,
and
.BR ft ;
.IP \(bu 2
the escapes
.B \ef
for fonts
.BR B ,
.BR I ,
.BR R ,
.BR P ,
and
.B 1
through
.BR 4 ,
where any other name is bold if it contains
.BR B ,
italic if it contains
.BR I ,
and roman otherwise, so that
.B CB
is bold and
.B CW
roman;
.BR \e* ,
.BR \en ,
.BR \ew ,
.BR \ek ,
.BR \eh ,
.BR \et ,
.BR \ea ,
and
.BR \ec ;
the glyphs
\fB\e(\fIxx\fR,
\fB\e[\fIname\fB]\fR,
and
\fB\eC\(aq\fIxx\fB\(aq\fR
from a table of about 300 of groff's glyphs, and
\fB\e[u\fIXXXX\fB]\fR
and
\fB\eN\(aq\fIn\fB\(aq\fR
by code point; the literal characters
.BR \e\- ,
.BR \ee ,
.BR \eE ,
.BR \e\e ,
.BR \e. ,
.BR \e\(aq ,
and
.BR \e\(ga ,
where
.B \e\(aq
is an acute accent with
.BR \-Tutf8 ;
and
.B \e&
and
.BR \e) ,
which print nothing.
Any other undefined single-character escape prints that character, and
an unknown glyph prints nothing.
.PP
The conditions
.BR n ,
.BR o ,
and
.B c
are true, and
.BR t ,
.BR v ,
.BR e ,
.BR h ,
.BR m ,
.BR F ,
and
.B S
are false.
.B so
resolves a relative path against the current directory, and skips a
file it cannot read.
Tab stops set by
.B ta
are all left-aligned and do not repeat.
The predefined registers describe groff 1.23 on a terminal:
.B .g
is 1, and the registers for the line length, indent, fill and
adjustment modes, font, input line, and argument count follow the
current state.
The register
.B an\-margin
holds the man(7) left margin, which
.B RS
and
.B RE
move.
.B \ek
marks a position that only later input lines can read, since
.B \en
and the other interpolations are done before a line is formatted.
.SS man(7)
The structure macros
.BR TH ,
.BR SH ,
.BR SS ,
.BR PP ,
.BR LP ,
.BR P ,
.BR TP ,
.BR TQ ,
.BR IP ,
.BR HP ,
.BR RS ,
.BR RE ,
and
.BR PD ;
the font macros
.BR B ,
.BR I ,
.BR SM ,
and
.BR SB ,
which apply to the next line when given no arguments, and
.BR BR ,
.BR BI ,
.BR IB ,
.BR IR ,
.BR RB ,
and
.BR RI ;
and
.BR UR ,
.BR UE ,
.BR MT ,
.BR ME ,
.BR EX ,
.BR EE ,
.BR SY ,
.BR YS ,
.BR OP ,
.BR MR ,
and
.BR DT .
.SS tbl
Tables between
.B .TS
and
.BR .TE ,
with
.BR .T& ;
the options
.BR tab() ,
.BR box ,
.BR frame ,
.BR doublebox ,
.BR doubleframe ,
.BR allbox ,
.BR center ,
.BR centre ,
and
.BR expand ,
where the double forms draw a single frame;
the column formats
.BR l ,
.BR r ,
.BR c ,
.BR n ,
.BR a ,
.BR s ,
.BR ^ ,
.BR _ ,
.BR \- ,
.BR = ,
and
.BR | ,
in either case, with the modifiers
.BR b ,
.BR i ,
.BR f ,
.BR t ,
.BR e ,
.BR x ,
.BR w() ,
and a number giving the column separation;
text blocks in
.B T{
and
.BR T} ,
which may hold requests;
the entries
.BR _ ,
.BR = ,
.BR \e_ ,
.BR \e^ ,
and
\fB\eR\fIx\fR;
rule rows of
.B _
or
.BR = ,
as data lines or as format rows; and
.B .sp
between rows.
Other options, the modifiers
.BR u ,
.BR z ,
.BR d ,
.BR p ,
and
.BR v ,
and other requests between rows are ignored.
A malformed format, or one with more than 64 columns or 256 rows, is
read as data, and every entry is left-aligned.
Rules are drawn with box-drawing characters with
.BR \-Tutf8 ,
and with
.BR \- ,
.BR = ,
.BR | ,
and
.B +
otherwise.
.SS mdoc(7)
The prologue
.BR Dd ,
.BR Dt ,
and
.BR Os ;
.B Sh
and
.BR Ss ;
lists with
.B Bl
in all its forms, with
.BR \-width ,
.BR \-offset ,
and
.BR \-compact ;
displays with
.B Bd
.BR \-literal ,
.BR \-unfilled ,
.BR \-filled ,
and
.BR \-ragged ,
with
.B \-offset
and
.BR \-compact ,
where
.B \-centered
is filled but not centered;
.BR D1 ,
.BR Dl ,
enclosures,
.BR Xo / Xc ,
.BR Bk / Ek ,
and
.BR Rs / Re ;
the common inline macros, such as
.BR Nm ,
.BR Fl ,
.BR Ar ,
.BR Op ,
.BR Xr ,
and
.BR Fn ;
.B Ex
and
.B Rv
with
.BR \-std ;
.BR St ;
and the system names, such as
.B At
and
.BR Bx .
Requests and tables work in mdoc(7) pages as in man(7) pages.
.SS Ignored input
These requests, among others, are accepted and ignored:
.BR hy ,
.BR nh ,
.BR hw ,
.BR ne ,
.BR po ,
.BR bp ,
.BR ps ,
.BR vs ,
.BR cs ,
.BR fam ,
.BR tm ,
.BR it ,
.BR ftr ,
.BR lf ,
.BR cc ,
.BR ec ,
.BR di ,
.BR ev ,
.BR wh ,
and
.BR while .
Input between
.B .EQ
and
.BR .EN ,
and between
.B .PS
and
.BR .PE ,
is skipped.
.B mso
is ignored, except that loading
.I www.tmac
defines its
.B URL
and
.B MTO
link macros.
The man(7) macros
.BR AT ,
.BR UC ,
.BR PT ,
.BR BT ,
.BR HY ,
and
.B LINKSTYLE
are ignored, and the mdoc(7) macros
.BR Bt ,
.BR Ud ,
.BR Hf ,
.BR Es ,
and
.B En
print only their arguments.
Unknown requests and macros are ignored.
The escapes
.BR \es ,
.BR \ev ,
.BR \eu ,
.BR \ed ,
.BR \er ,
.BR \em ,
.BR \eM ,
.BR \eg ,
.BR \ej ,
.BR \ep ,
.BR \eF ,
.BR \eV ,
.BR \eY ,
.BR \eA ,
.BR \eB ,
.BR \eR ,
.BR \eS ,
.BR \eH ,
.BR \eb ,
.BR \ex ,
.BR \el ,
.BR \eL ,
.BR \eD ,
.BR \eX ,
and
.B \eZ
are parsed and dropped;
.BR \e| ,
.BR \e^ ,
.BR \e, ,
and
.B \e/
produce nothing;
.B \e:
is a break point, and
.B \e%
within a word is a break point that adds a hyphen;
.B \e
followed by a space,
.BR \e~ ,
and
.B \e0
are unbreakable spaces; and
.B \eo
and
.B \ez
are approximated.
.SH OPTIONS
Options are not grouped.
The arguments of
.BR \-T ,
.BR \-r ,
and
.B \-d
may be attached or separate, and
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.TP
.BR \-man ", " \-mandoc ", " \-mdoc
Ignored, as is any other
.B \-m
option.
The macro package is detected from the input.
.TP
.B \-t
Ignored, since
.B tbl
support is built in.
.TP
.BI \-P arg
Ignored.
The argument, which groff passes to its output driver, must be attached.
.TP
.B \-Tascii
Write ASCII: glyphs and common non-ASCII characters are approximated,
and other non-ASCII input is printed as
.BR ? .
This is the default, and any other device name selects it.
.TP
.BR \-Tutf8 ", " \-Tutf\-8
Write UTF-8: glyphs are Unicode characters, and UTF-8 input passes
through, except that an invalid byte is printed as
.BR ? .
.TP
\fB\-rLL=\fIN\fBn\fR
Set the line length to
.I N
columns.
The default is
.BR MANWIDTH ,
or
.BR COLUMNS ,
or 78.
A length outside 10 to 1000 columns is ignored, here and in those
variables.
.TP
\fB\-r\fIreg\fB=\fIN\fR
Set the number register
.I reg
to
.IR N ,
for the page to test.
Except for
.BR \-rLL ,
which sets the line length and no register, this does not affect the
layout: header and footer lines use the line length, so
.BR \-rLT ,
which
.BR man (1)
passes, has no effect.
.TP
\fB\-d\fIname\fB=\fIstring\fR
Define the string
.I name
as
.IR string ,
as
.B .ds
does.
Without
.BR = ,
the first character is the name and the rest is the string, as in
groff.
A man(7) page reads the string
.B AD
at
.B .TH
and at each paragraph macro to choose its adjustment, as groff does:
.B l
sets text ragged-right,
.B c
centers it,
.B r
aligns it right, and anything else, the default, adjusts it to both
margins.
.BR man (1)
passes this option from
.BR MANROFFOPT ,
so
.B MANROFFOPT=\-dAD=l
gives ragged-right pages.
.TP
.B \-w
Warn on standard error, with file names and line numbers, about unknown
macros, requests, escapes, and glyphs, undefined strings,
.B .so
files that cannot be read, and macro calls or
.B .so
files nested too deeply.
Requests and escapes that are accepted and ignored draw no warning.
This checks pages as they are written.
Any option beginning with
.B \-w
does the same, and one beginning with
.B \-W
turns warnings off.
Warnings do not change the exit status.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary on standard output and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MANWIDTH
The line length, unless
.B \-rLL
gives one.
.TP
.B COLUMNS
The line length, unless
.B \-rLL
or
.B MANWIDTH
gives one.
.TP
.B GROFF_NO_SGR
When set, even to an empty value, write no escape sequences for bold
and italic text.
.BR man (1)
sets it when its output is not a terminal.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A
.I file
or a
.B .so
file could not be read, or the output could not be written.
.TP
.B 2
Bad usage, or out of memory.
.PP
A write error prints no message, since it normally means the pager was
closed.
.SH EXAMPLES
View a page that is not installed:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ nroff page.1 | less \-R
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check a page for constructs
.B nroff
does not recognize:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ nroff \-w page.1 >/dev/null
.EE
.RE
.PP
Save a page as plain UTF-8 text, 72 columns wide:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ GROFF_NO_SGR=1 nroff \-Tutf8 \-rLL=72n page.1 >page.txt
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
.B nroff
supports only what manual pages use, and is not a general roff
implementation: it has no traps, diversions, environments, or output
other than a terminal.
.PP
Limits bound runaway input.
Macro calls and
.B .so
files nest at most 64 deep, and strings 32 deep.
A run reads at most 1048576 input lines, counting each line of a macro
body every time it runs and each string interpolation, and silently
drops the rest.
Formatting many pages in one run can reach this.
An output line holds at most 4096 columns, and text beyond is dropped.
Space requested between two output lines is at most 100 blank lines.
.B nroff
allocates 256 MiB of memory, 128 MiB as a 32-bit program, or 32 MiB
when that much is unavailable, and exits with status 2 when it runs out.
.PP
.BR man (1)
passes
.B \-Tutf8
when its ANSI code page is UTF-8, and
.B \-Tascii
otherwise.
It runs
.B nroff
from the root of the manual tree that holds the page, so
.B .so
paths such as
.I man1/gcc.1
resolve, or from the current directory for a page named by its path.
.PP
Through
.B less
on a console without virtual terminal support, as on Windows XP, bold
text shows but underlining does not.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR less (1),
.BR man (1)
